## Linked List Operations

A `LinkedList[T]` is a doubly-linked list. Unlike Vec, inserting and removing at the front or middle is O(1) — no shifting required.

### Creating and Using

```xiom
let list = LinkedList[Int]();
list.push_back(10);
list.push_back(20);
list.push_back(30);
list.push_front(5);  // add to front
// list: 5 <-> 10 <-> 20 <-> 30
```

### Removing Elements

```xiom
var list = LinkedList[Int]();
list.push_back(1); list.push_back(2); list.push_back(3);

let first = list.pop_front();  // Some(1)
let last = list.pop_back();    // Some(3)
// list now: [2]
```

### LinkedList vs Vec

| Operation | LinkedList | Vec |
|-----------|-----------|-----|
| Push back | O(1) | O(1)* |
| Push front | O(1) | O(n) |
| Pop front | O(1) | O(n) |
| Random access | O(n) | O(1) |
| Memory | Per-node allocation | Contiguous |

### When to Use LinkedList

- **Queue**: push_back + pop_front
- **Deque**: both ends operations
- **Frequent front insertions**: avoid Vec's O(n) penalty

```xiom
// Queue pattern with LinkedList
fn enqueue(q: &mut LinkedList[Int], item: Int) -> Unit {
  q.push_back(item);
}

fn dequeue(q: &mut LinkedList[Int]) -> Option[Int] {
  return q.pop_front();
}
```

### Iteration

```xiom
let list = LinkedList[Int]();
list.push_back(1); list.push_back(2); list.push_back(3);

for item in list {
  io.println(item);
}
```

### len and is_empty

```xiom
let list = LinkedList[Int]();
let empty = list.is_empty();  // true
list.push_back(42);
let has_item = list.is_empty();  // false
let size = list.len();  // 1
```

